Corrected Legendre odd address geometry at N=15
==============================================================================
Slots:
  A:N-1=14 +B:N-1=14 -C:N-2=13 +D:N-2=13 -E:N-3=12 -F:N-3=12 +G:N-4=11
Regions:
  corner_A : A                      sizes={14: 1}
  corner_D : D                      sizes={13: 1}
  corner_B : B                      sizes={14: 1}
  edge_AD  : A +D -E                sizes={14: 1, 13: 1, 12: -1}
  edge_BD  : B +D -F                sizes={14: 1, 13: 1, 12: -1}
  edge_AB  : A +B -C                sizes={14: 2, 13: -1}
  center   : A +B -C +D -E -F +G    sizes={14: 2, 13: 0, 12: -2, 11: 1}

Key point:
  C and D are both size N-2, so they cancel in scalar cardinality,
  but they are different addresses: C sits on the AB edge, D is the
  third corner and feeds both AD and BD edges plus the center.

LRC14 composition: two size coordinates must be kept
==============================================================================
At N=14, h(N)=42 = 7*6 is pronic.
  Mobius skeleton slots:     A:N-1=13 +B:N-1=13 +C:N-1=13 -D:N-2=12 -E:N-2=12 -F:N-2=12 +G:N-3=11
  Eisenstein even slots:     A:N-1=13 +B:N-1=13 -C:N-2=12
  Shape/apex coordinate:     k=N/2=7, the odd Legendre character coordinate.

Do not conflate these coordinates:
  recurrence children of N=14 are sizes 13 and 12;
  the apex-7 coordinate is the pronic fold parameter k=7;
  the Legendre chart at apex size 7 has slots
    A:N-1=6 +B:N-1=6 -C:N-2=5 +D:N-2=5 -E:N-3=4 -F:N-3=4 +G:N-4=3
  while the odd half chart at N=15 has slots
    A:N-1=14 +B:N-1=14 -C:N-2=13 +D:N-2=13 -E:N-3=12 -F:N-3=12 +G:N-4=11

Thus composition means a parity-stratified address sheaf:
  every node carries the Mobius full atlas;
  even half nodes carry the Eisenstein pronic quotient;
  odd half nodes carry the Legendre square/three-corner quotient;
  scalarizing before matching these local addresses loses the C/D seam.

Exact-period obstruction for S_X={1..11,13,lcm(2..X)}
==============================================================================
For every D<=X, lcm(2..X) is 0 mod D, so N(S_X,D)=0 exactly.
The scan below corrects the stronger nextprime wording: q_min>X is
rigorous and sufficient to refute any finite basis; q_min need not be
nextprime(X) because the fixed AP-core has its own safe-denominator ladder.

  X   nextprime(X)  first witness D  first safe units
  14             17 41               [17, 24]
  15             17 41               [17, 24]
  16             17 41               [17, 24]
  20             23 41               [17, 24]
  24             29 53               [22, 31]
  27             29 41               [17, 24]
  30             31 41               [17, 24]
  41             43 53               [22, 31]
  60             61 67               [28, 39]
  90             97 97               [8, 89]

Tournament Analysis over proof carriers
==============================================================================
  vertices are proof carriers, not runners or arcs
  observable=(labels preserved, common labels, declaration order)
  score_hist={0: 1, 1: 1, 2: 1, 3: 1, 4: 1, 5: 1, 6: 1}
  directed_3cycles=0
  Hamiltonian path=exact_period_packets > address_sheaf > legendre_CD_seam > eisenstein_pronic_fold > mobius_scalar_skeleton > three_gap_AP_hull > raw_runner_vertices

Assumption challenged:
  A scalar half-tile recurrence is not enough to preserve the LRC
  predicate.  The quotient must retain exact-period packets, local
  slot labels, and parity mode until the final cap/floor scalarization.

Proof-route synthesis
==============================================================================
  Node 2 remains finite/algebraic: prove AP-hull or three-gap
  majorization for the bounded cap side without losing sector labels.
  Node 3 is analytic: lcm-loaded rows force unbounded witness
  denominators, so Weyl/equidistribution or exact-period packet
  density is irreducible.
  The three recursion modes compose as an address system connecting
  these branches, not as a closed scalar recurrence for witness counts.
